WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.868
-Our next guest is CNN's
chief political correspondent

00:00:02.868 --> 00:00:04.604
and co-anchor
of "State of the Union,"

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:07.040
which airs Sunday mornings
at 9:00 on CNN.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.542
Please welcome back to the show,
Dana Bash.

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:12.078
♪♪

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:14.580
-Hi!
[ Applause ]

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.082
Nice to see you in person.

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:17.550
-I know.
Thank you for making the trip.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.385
-My pleasure.
Thank you.

00:00:19.385 --> 00:00:22.622
♪♪

00:00:22.622 --> 00:00:24.023
-This is very nice.

00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:26.025
Last time I saw you
was on a Zoom screen.

00:00:26.025 --> 00:00:27.193
-I know.
I know.

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:28.827
We're inching,
inching back to normalcy.

00:00:28.827 --> 00:00:30.396
-Well, then, a very
interesting thing, though.

00:00:30.396 --> 00:00:32.231
The tables were turned.
You came here,

00:00:32.231 --> 00:00:34.033
and I think a first for us --
you were just

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:35.735
on a CNN Zoom screen.
-I was.

00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:37.670
-You did a live hit
from our dressing room.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.038
-I did.
-Because, you know,

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:40.340
we tape this early in the day.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.876
Joe Biden has just spoken
while we are talking here.

00:00:43.876 --> 00:00:48.347
What was your reaction,
not just to the speed in which

00:00:48.347 --> 00:00:50.116
the Taliban has taken back
the country

00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:52.118
but Joe Biden's remarks today?

00:00:52.118 --> 00:00:55.388
-They were very focused on what
you mentioned in your open,

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:59.426
which is that Americans are done
with this 20-year war, right?

00:00:59.426 --> 00:01:02.295
And so it was
for domestic consumption.

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:06.565
It was making the case that,
as bad and as hard as this is,

00:01:06.565 --> 00:01:09.502
that if it didn't happen now,
it could've happened

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:12.238
five years ago,
if the U.S. pulled out then,

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:14.974
or 15 years ago
if the U.S. stays longer.

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:18.645
But it doesn't change the fact
that we still don't know why

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:21.681
the execution
seemed so bungled, right?

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:23.650
-Yeah.
-I mean, the fact that we have

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:27.053
these images, but the images
tell the story of people

00:01:27.053 --> 00:01:29.822
who were asking to get out
and knew this deadline

00:01:29.822 --> 00:01:33.526
was coming
and didn't get approval

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:37.630
or didn't get the green light
and just didn't get taken out

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.398
before this happened.
-Yeah.

00:01:39.398 --> 00:01:41.267
Because there's been
a lot of reporting

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:44.103
of people in Afghanistan
knew this day were coming.

00:01:44.103 --> 00:01:45.804
You know,
this special immigrant visa.

00:01:45.804 --> 00:01:48.407
18,000 people, at least,
who are backlogged.

00:01:48.407 --> 00:01:50.910
So they knew it was coming.
And I think another thing

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.446
to keep in mind is,
I don't think a lot Americans,

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:55.582
day-to-day,
think about Afghanistan.

00:01:55.582 --> 00:01:57.783
-Definitely not.
-But we now have these images

00:01:57.783 --> 00:02:00.853
that are sort of indelible
as to what we're seeing.

00:02:00.853 --> 00:02:05.391
And yet we also have
these bad-faith arguments

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:08.394
by those on the right
who are forgetting that the plan

00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:09.628
that is being carried out
right now

00:02:09.628 --> 00:02:12.365
was Donald Trump's plan.
-He put it in place.

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:15.534
He set the deadline.
Well, let me back up.

00:02:15.534 --> 00:02:20.506
He made the agreement inside
Afghanistan for the pullout.

00:02:20.506 --> 00:02:23.576
He pulled
most of the troops out,

00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:26.579
save for 2,500,
and then set the deadline.

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:29.682
So the only question for
Joe Biden when he took office

00:02:29.682 --> 00:02:33.653
is whether or not he would
stand by that or change things.

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:37.857
And when he was Vice President,
he, I guess now famously,

00:02:37.857 --> 00:02:43.229
was a very minority voice
within the Obama administration

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:46.499
telling then President Obama,
"We should get out now."

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:49.435
And President Obama
ultimately decided to stay.

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:52.605
But it is just a reminder
that now President Biden

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:55.341
has wanted to get out of
Afghanistan for quite some time.

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:57.977
And that's what he basically
said in his speech,

00:02:57.977 --> 00:03:00.380
that we need to end this,
and we need to focus,

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:04.150
as Americans, on threats
that are much more grave,

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:07.920
much more pressing,
like China and like Russia.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.189
-You reported
from inside Afghanistan

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:13.393
at a time when there
maybe was some optimism.

00:03:13.393 --> 00:03:14.794
-It was so different, Seth.

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:17.530
I was with
then Vice President Cheney.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.431
First we made a surprise trip
to Iraq,

00:03:19.431 --> 00:03:20.899
which is
a whole different story.

00:03:20.899 --> 00:03:23.002
But then,
we went to Afghanistan,

00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:25.304
and the reason he was there,
it was because

00:03:25.304 --> 00:03:27.440
it was the opening
of the General Assembly.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.043
And so it was
totally bizarre to go in.

00:03:31.043 --> 00:03:33.313
I was part
of a very small press pool.

00:03:33.313 --> 00:03:35.581
But he went in,
and then we followed

00:03:35.581 --> 00:03:38.784
to a very small,
smoke-filled room.

00:03:38.784 --> 00:03:41.187
But there were elected members

00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:44.857
of the Parliament
inside Afghanistan.

00:03:44.857 --> 00:03:47.626
And there were women there,
there were members

00:03:47.626 --> 00:03:50.663
of the Taliban who were elected,
sitting side by side.

00:03:50.663 --> 00:03:53.599
And I remember thinking,
"Wow! This is amazing,"

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:55.768
and wondering
if it was fleeting.

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:59.738
And the answer is yes,
because it is, as you said,

00:03:59.738 --> 00:04:02.207
hubris for America,
time and time again,

00:04:02.207 --> 00:04:07.846
to try to impose ideals
and a philosophy on a place

00:04:07.846 --> 00:04:09.415
that just
isn't interested in it,

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:12.518
and that was proved
by the fact that 300,000 --

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:16.989
or however many Afghan troops
were trained, and they,

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:19.458
for whatever reason,
just didn't have the will --

00:04:19.458 --> 00:04:20.859
obviously,
there was fear in there,

00:04:20.859 --> 00:04:22.695
but they didn't
have the will to fight.

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:24.564
-You --
We talked about it.

00:04:24.564 --> 00:04:26.699
You reported in Afghanistan.
We're very lucky right now

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:28.334
to have people reporting
on the ground,

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:31.103
which talking about fear,
you know, I will say

00:04:31.103 --> 00:04:33.739
the safety of my desk is about
as much as I would risk.

00:04:33.739 --> 00:04:35.474
-You and me both.
-And your colleague,

00:04:35.474 --> 00:04:37.509
Clarissa Ward,
who's been doing reporting

00:04:37.509 --> 00:04:39.412
from inside the country, and,
you know, there is sort of

00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:41.714
an 8-second clip
that's going around right now,

00:04:41.714 --> 00:04:43.849
as well as a meme
that she's addressed.

00:04:43.849 --> 00:04:46.986
And, again,
these are bad-faith actors,

00:04:46.986 --> 00:04:48.788
both elected officials
and in the media,

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:51.658
who are trying to frame
what she said.

00:04:51.658 --> 00:04:54.060
How upsetting is it to you
that -- especially when they're

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.763
attacking the people
that are doing the sort of,

00:04:56.763 --> 00:04:58.130
you know, the boot leather,

00:04:58.130 --> 00:04:59.565
actually being there
in real time.

00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:02.101
-I don't even have words to
describe how upsetting it is,

00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:04.937
because you're exactly right.

00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:07.407
She is there.
She is on the ground.

00:05:07.407 --> 00:05:09.942
She is reporting, real time,
what is happening

00:05:09.942 --> 00:05:12.511
in the most dangerous
of circumstances

00:05:12.511 --> 00:05:14.681
for anybody of any gender,

00:05:14.681 --> 00:05:16.749
and here she is
as a woman doing it

00:05:16.749 --> 00:05:21.287
and confronting members of the
Taliban, who said to her --

00:05:21.287 --> 00:05:24.423
she was covered, but she needed
to be covered more

00:05:24.423 --> 00:05:25.991
because you could
still see her face.

00:05:25.991 --> 00:05:27.192
She needed to be covered more

00:05:27.192 --> 00:05:29.062
because you could
still see her fingers --

00:05:29.062 --> 00:05:30.496
she needed to be wearing gloves.

00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:34.399
And she's doing her reporting
for everybody in the world,

00:05:34.399 --> 00:05:40.106
particularly Americans,
with these kinds of dangers

00:05:40.106 --> 00:05:41.740
and these kinds of realities.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:44.309
And for all the people
who criticize her,

00:05:44.309 --> 00:05:47.813
I'd like them to go spend
5 seconds in her shoes,

00:05:47.813 --> 00:05:52.118
never mind five minutes,
or five days and -- or months,

00:05:52.118 --> 00:05:53.920
which is what
she has done, combined.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:56.522
She's such a badass, Seth.
I mean, I can't say --

00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:58.958
And by the way, not for nothing,
she has two babies at home.

00:05:58.958 --> 00:06:01.460
She's a mom.
-It is -- it is truly --

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:03.329
truly impressive.
Although I have two kids

00:06:03.329 --> 00:06:04.763
at home,
and there are times where

00:06:04.763 --> 00:06:06.232
that's when I want
to go to Afghanistan.

00:06:06.232 --> 00:06:07.432
[ Laughter ]

00:06:07.432 --> 00:06:08.567
-I hear you.
-Yeah. You know --

00:06:08.567 --> 00:06:09.901
You know the feeling,
where you're like,

00:06:09.901 --> 00:06:11.970
"I'm gonna go somewhere
a little bit more low-key."

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:13.305
-Um --
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.305 --> 00:06:15.508
So, I want to talk to you --
the last time we spoke,

00:06:15.508 --> 00:06:16.842
you were with
a couple of your colleagues.

00:06:16.842 --> 00:06:19.746
And now a lot has changed
even since then.

00:06:19.746 --> 00:06:20.846
You're co-hosting
"State of the Union."

00:06:20.846 --> 00:06:23.816
-Mm-hmm.
-Your career began

00:06:23.816 --> 00:06:27.553
when the news -- technology
was certainly different.

00:06:27.553 --> 00:06:29.154
-Yeah.
-What were your early days

00:06:29.154 --> 00:06:30.422
in journalism?

00:06:30.422 --> 00:06:32.624
-Well, I started at CNN
right out of college.

00:06:32.624 --> 00:06:34.893
In fact, I was still in school.

00:06:34.893 --> 00:06:36.729
It was my second semester
of my senior year.

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:39.298
I was living in Washington,
and I started freelancing.

00:06:39.298 --> 00:06:43.168
And my first job job
I got right on my 22nd birthday.

00:06:43.168 --> 00:06:46.072
And -- Which is in the summer,
right after I graduated.

00:06:46.072 --> 00:06:48.775
And it was
to work in a tape library.

00:06:48.775 --> 00:06:51.277
So that means
that there was videotape.

00:06:51.277 --> 00:06:54.180
-Yeah.
-And there was a library for --

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.816
to house that videotape,
and none of that exists anymore.

00:06:56.816 --> 00:06:58.484
I mean, we don't do videotape,
right?

00:06:58.484 --> 00:07:00.319
It's all --
And so when I tell people that,

00:07:00.319 --> 00:07:02.354
they're like, "What is that?"
I said, "You know the thing

00:07:02.354 --> 00:07:04.090
that you see in the movies,
where they put the --

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:05.591
the, you know --
Go back and watch

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:07.326
'Broadcast News,'" which also
the kids don't know what it is.

00:07:07.326 --> 00:07:08.427
-[ Laughing ] Exactly.
-But that's what --

00:07:08.427 --> 00:07:09.661
But that's basically
what it was.

00:07:09.661 --> 00:07:11.197
That was the technology
back then.

00:07:11.197 --> 00:07:13.398
-I mean, I --
I worked as an intern

00:07:13.398 --> 00:07:15.001
for Comedy Central
in the mid '90s,

00:07:15.001 --> 00:07:17.035
and my job was literally
running videotapes --

00:07:17.035 --> 00:07:18.704
getting in taxis
and running videotapes

00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:20.839
to other parts of the city.
It was --

00:07:20.839 --> 00:07:22.475
And it's crazy to think back.
-That's amazing. Yeah.

00:07:22.475 --> 00:07:24.677
-'Cause, again,
you just can't -- you --

00:07:24.677 --> 00:07:27.647
None of us can conceive
of the fact that our 20s

00:07:27.647 --> 00:07:29.148
will be ancient history.
You know?

00:07:29.148 --> 00:07:31.316
-Ancient, ancient history.
I mean, to think

00:07:31.316 --> 00:07:34.186
that we didn't have --
I mean, we barely had e-mail.

00:07:34.186 --> 00:07:36.155
-Yeah.
-We barely had the Internet.

00:07:36.155 --> 00:07:37.222
We barely had any of that.

00:07:37.222 --> 00:07:38.757
God, I feel like
I'm 100 years old.

00:07:38.757 --> 00:07:40.225
I should stop talking.
-Yeah, we should stop.

00:07:40.225 --> 00:07:43.262
Let's talk about someone
that young people like -- AOC.

00:07:43.262 --> 00:07:45.164
-Yeah.
-You did -- you did a --

00:07:45.164 --> 00:07:46.799
basically an hour special
with her.

00:07:46.799 --> 00:07:48.734
And you've obviously interviewed

00:07:48.734 --> 00:07:51.203
and spent time
with many politicians.

00:07:51.203 --> 00:07:53.472
I don't think I'm alone
in making the comment

00:07:53.472 --> 00:07:54.707
that there's
something unique about her.

00:07:54.707 --> 00:07:56.775
What did you find
in your time with her?

00:07:56.775 --> 00:07:58.311
-There is. And, you know,
you had a cameo...

00:07:58.311 --> 00:07:59.711
-I was very happy
to make a cameo. Thank you.

00:07:59.711 --> 00:08:02.414
-...in that hour,
because, when I was doing my --

00:08:02.414 --> 00:08:04.717
my research, I watched
the interview that she did.

00:08:04.717 --> 00:08:06.352
She's sitting right here
with you,

00:08:06.352 --> 00:08:08.087
and there was
such a classic moment.

00:08:08.087 --> 00:08:10.489
I was trying to figure out
how to get at the fact

00:08:10.489 --> 00:08:13.259
that one of the reasons
she is so polarizing,

00:08:13.259 --> 00:08:15.861
and in particular,
people on the right

00:08:15.861 --> 00:08:19.498
are so fixated on her,
is because she's so beautiful,

00:08:19.498 --> 00:08:21.734
and she's so young,
and they don't really know

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:24.270
what to do with her,
and you just nailed it.

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:28.273
You just went at it in
a very, very clever way.

00:08:28.273 --> 00:08:30.709
So instead of me figuring out
how to ask the question,

00:08:30.709 --> 00:08:32.144
I just put it right on you.
-Thank you.

00:08:32.144 --> 00:08:35.147
Look, any time I can find my way
into stuff on other networks,

00:08:35.147 --> 00:08:36.848
'cause I'm trying to, like,
spread it out, you know?

00:08:36.848 --> 00:08:38.017
Yeah.
-Yeah. Well, yeah.

00:08:38.017 --> 00:08:40.052
It was very welcomed.
-But, there is something

00:08:40.052 --> 00:08:42.655
about her that I feel like
you pointed out,

00:08:42.655 --> 00:08:46.925
which is that she's also not
ashamed of those things.

00:08:46.925 --> 00:08:49.628
I feel like there -- you know,
there's this long history

00:08:49.628 --> 00:08:52.899
of women politicians having to,
you know, like,

00:08:52.899 --> 00:08:55.768
bend themselves towards
what we perceive

00:08:55.768 --> 00:08:58.270
as strengths
of male politicians.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:00.873
And she sort of unapologetically
doesn't do that.

00:09:00.873 --> 00:09:05.344
-My favorite segment in this
hour that we did was about that,

00:09:05.344 --> 00:09:09.281
which is that she said,
"There is power in femininity,

00:09:09.281 --> 00:09:11.817
and that's why I own it."

00:09:11.817 --> 00:09:15.021
I mean, she did a tutorial
for "Vogue" magazine

00:09:15.021 --> 00:09:16.422
where she did
her whole beauty routine,

00:09:16.422 --> 00:09:18.591
including her famous red lips.

00:09:18.591 --> 00:09:22.761
And not a lot of women
politicians would ever do that,

00:09:22.761 --> 00:09:25.798
and the reason is because,
for the most part,

00:09:25.798 --> 00:09:28.400
women conform to the idea
of male power,

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:30.502
and that's the argument
that she was making.

00:09:30.502 --> 00:09:33.139
And it's not even something
that ever occurred to me.

00:09:33.139 --> 00:09:36.876
And I think it's generational,
and -- and it's very unique

00:09:36.876 --> 00:09:38.577
to her, which is
part of the conversation

00:09:38.577 --> 00:09:40.345
that I thought
was so interesting.

00:09:40.345 --> 00:09:41.980
-Well, it's always interesting
talking to you.

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:43.916
I really do appreciate you
making the trip in person.

00:09:43.916 --> 00:09:45.017
-I'm so glad to be here.

00:09:45.017 --> 00:09:46.518
-And hopefully
we'll see you soon.

00:09:46.518 --> 00:09:48.520
-Yeah. Thank you.
-You guys, "State of the Union"

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:51.390
airs Sundays at 9:00 a.m.
and noon on CNN.

